# @opentelemetry/core 1.30.1 pinned inside @vscode/extension-telemetry > applicationinsights (1.x SDK tree).
# No patched 1.x release exists (fix is only 2.8.0); the 2.x copies are already resolved to 2.8.0.
# CVE-2026-54285 is unbounded memory allocation while PARSING an inbound W3C Baggage header. This
# extension only EMITS outbound telemetry to Azure and never parses attacker-controlled baggage headers,
# so the vulnerable code path is not reachable. Re-evaluate when applicationinsights ships OTel 2.x.
# exp:2026-09-18
CVE-2026-54285

# brace-expansion DoS/OOM on hostile brace patterns. The 5.x line is already bumped to the patched 5.0.8.
# The two copies left are 1.1.16 (devDependency @vscode/vsce > minimatch@3) and 2.1.2 (devDependency
# mocha > minimatch@9 / glob@10 > minimatch@9). No patched 1.x or 2.x release exists, and 5.x is not a
# drop-in replacement (CommonJS build exports { expand } instead of module.exports = expand, while
# @vscode/vsce calls the module as a function), so neither an upgrade nor a scoped resolution is viable.
# The only patterns reaching vsce and mocha are this repo's own globs (.vscodeignore, package.json,
# .mocharc) - never untrusted input - and both parents are devDependency-only tooling that is not
# bundled into the shipped extension. Re-evaluate when @vscode/vsce and mocha move to minimatch@10.
# exp:2026-10-25
CVE-2026-14257

# --- mermaid 11.15.0 ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The five advisories below are fixed only in mermaid 11.16.1, and the vulnerable code is mermaid's own,
# so there is no sub-package to pin through resolutions. 11.16.x cannot be adopted: it regresses the
# DevOps Pipeline cluster layout (the Salesforce Orgs subgraph loses "direction TB" and the deployment
# links stop at the subgraph border, verified by headless rendering), which is why mermaid is pinned to
# exactly 11.15.0 in package.json. Shared input analysis: this extension renders exactly one diagram. Its
# source is generated by src/utils/pipeline/branchStrategyMermaidBuilder.ts (always "flowchart LR",
# preceded by our own %%{init}%% directive) and is passed to the single mermaid.render() call in
# src/webviews/lwc-ui/modules/s/pipeline/pipeline.js. No markdown / mermaid fence rendering exists
# anywhere in the extension, so no third-party diagram source is ever parsed.
# Both the CVE and the GHSA id are listed for each advisory, because Trivy reports npm findings under
# either identifier depending on the advisory source.
# Re-evaluate all five when mermaid ships a fix for the 11.16 layout regression.

# XY Charts DoS. Not reachable: only "flowchart LR" is ever emitted
# (branchStrategyMermaidBuilder.ts) and the diagram header line is ours, so the XY Charts parser is never
# entered.
# exp:2026-11-16
CVE-2026-71436
GHSA-6x64-9x62-f2gx

# Radar diagram "ticks" DoS. Not reachable, same proof: only "flowchart LR" is ever generated
# (branchStrategyMermaidBuilder.ts), so the Radar parser is never entered.
# exp:2026-11-16
CVE-2026-71439
GHSA-rhh3-jpg6-66xh

# Prototype pollution through the mermaid config APIs. Not reachable: mermaid.initialize() is called
# exactly once, in src/webviews/lwc-ui-panel.ts, with a literal config object whose only variable parts
# are two theme colors picked from a hardcoded light/dark table. No user, workspace or remote data ever
# reaches a mermaid config API.
# exp:2026-11-16
CVE-2026-71438
GHSA-c4c3-pg64-4m4v

# Prototype pollution reachable from diagram input. Accepted risk, NOT claimed unreachable: the diagram
# structure is ours, but node labels embed branch names and org aliases verbatim
# (branchStrategyMermaidBuilder.ts), and for open pull requests the source branch name comes from the git
# provider, so on a public repository a fork PR author influences part of the diagram text. Accepted
# because exploitation requires the developer to open the DevOps Pipeline panel on a repository with
# hostile PR branch names, the panel is a local read-only webview, and the diagram type line plus the
# %%{init}%% directive are emitted by us rather than by the input.
# exp:2026-11-16
CVE-2026-71437
GHSA-3rrr-jr9j-h3q3

# CSS injection through diagram input. Accepted risk, NOT claimed unreachable - same input analysis as
# CVE-2026-71437: labels are interpolated raw and mermaid runs with securityLevel 'loose' (required for
# the clickable pull request links carried in edge labels). Accepted because the impact is bounded to
# restyling the local Pipeline webview: CSS cannot execute script, and the panel renders no credentials,
# tokens or org access data.
# exp:2026-11-16
CVE-2026-50159
GHSA-2v8p-3f2j-5mp7
